.decl Start(node:symbol)
.input Start
.decl Edge(src_node:symbol, dst_node:symbol, weight:number)
.input Edge
.decl ShortestPath(node:symbol, weight:number)
.output ShortestPath

.decl Bound(b:number)
.decl Dist(node:symbol, distance:number)

// The bound is what makes the recursion terminate, and it has to be a real
// upper bound rather than a stand-in for infinity. A shortest path uses each
// edge at most once, so the sum of all weights bounds every distance worth
// keeping; anything longer is discarded rather than carried forward.
//
// The previous reference instead seeded every non-source node with that same
// sum as a pretend "infinity" and then relaxed it like an ordinary distance.
// Two things went wrong. The sentinel could survive to the final round and be
// reported as a real answer -- a source with no outgoing edges yielded
// distances for nodes it cannot reach at all -- and a node whose predecessor
// had no value of its own dropped out of the scan entirely, so reachable nodes
// went missing. Against a true single-source shortest-path oracle it agreed on
// 29% of random acyclic graphs (1% when the source had no outgoing edges) and
// did not terminate on cyclic ones.
Bound(b) :- b = sum w : { Edge(_, _, w) }.

Dist(u, 0) :- Start(u).
Dist(v, d + w) :- Dist(u, d), Edge(u, v, w), Bound(b), d + w <= b.

ShortestPath(v, m) :- Dist(v, _), m = min d : Dist(v, d).
